{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1470804-met-rules-for-extended-actions","id":1470804,"title":"[MET] Rules for extended actions","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-12-18T13:14:44-08:00","id":1470804,"title":"<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/icons/icon5.png\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" alt=\"\" /> [MET] Rules for extended actions","content":"In tabletop Vampire, there are very clear rules for when a character wants to engage in an activity that takes place over a protracted period of time:<br /> <br /> 1. The player tells the ST what they want to accomplish.<br /> 2. The ST determines the number of success required to complete the action and sets the interval of time that each roll will represent.<br /> 3. The player assembles their dice pool and rolls until they have either achieved their desired results or botched so catastrophically that they cannot continue.<br /> <br /> So far as I can tell there are no rules in place for such &quot;Extended Challenges&quot; in Laws of the Night Revised. Is there a MET supplement book that details either conversion rules or similarly states how such things should be handled?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27140-doctor-awkward","name":"Doctor Awkward","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-18T19:49:05-08:00","id":1470815,"title":"","content":"Dark Epics pg. 70 has Extended Challenges but they're kind of awful. Basically you win the chop, then spend extra 'traits' to get the 'extended' result. It's poorly explained and implemented.<br /> <br /> You can basically do the same as tabletop, giving a 'success threshold' and treating each chop they win as a 'success,' and have those tests done for a particular timeframe. It works just the same as rolling dice for it, just need to account for the different failure things, retests if allowed and such.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1808-elmerg","name":"elmerg","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"539"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-18T22:23:46-08:00","id":1470823,"title":"","content":"I double-checked the writeup in the book and that's still not the same thing as an Extended Action. What is being described in the book is increasing degrees of success by spending extra traits. <br /> <br /> Here's an example of what I mean.<br /> I have a character with four dots in Craft: Automotive Mechanic and I want to make a car. I'm having all the necessary parts sent to me and I am essentially kitbashing it, so the ST tells me that it will be a difficulty 7 to assemble the parts together and I'll need to achieve 15 successes and each test represents one night of work. My Dexterity is 4 so I am rolling 8 dice. After 7 nights I have accumulated 18 successes so with a week of work I have assembled the car. Nice and simple.<br /> <br /> In a MET game if I have that same character and I want to engage in that same activity, I tell the ST that I am spending a downtime action and I chop... what? A Physical Challenge against 7 traits, Retest Crafts? And if I fail I wait a month to try again? How can there not even be guidelines in place to handle things like this?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27140-doctor-awkward","name":"Doctor Awkward","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-19T15:30:04-08:00","id":1470889,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Doctor Awkward</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1470804-met-rules-for-extended-actions?p=1470823#post1470823\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I double-checked the writeup in the book and that's still not the same thing as an Extended Action. What is being described in the book is increasing degrees of success by spending extra traits.<br /> <br /> Here's an example of what I mean.<br /> I have a character with four dots in Craft: Automotive Mechanic and I want to make a car. I'm having all the necessary parts sent to me and I am essentially kitbashing it, so the ST tells me that it will be a difficulty 7 to assemble the parts together and I'll need to achieve 15 successes and each test represents one night of work. My Dexterity is 4 so I am rolling 8 dice. After 7 nights I have accumulated 18 successes so with a week of work I have assembled the car. Nice and simple.<br /> <br /> In a MET game if I have that same character and I want to engage in that same activity, I tell the ST that I am spending a downtime action and I chop... what? A Physical Challenge against 7 traits, Retest Crafts? And if I fail I wait a month to try again? How can there not even be guidelines in place to handle things like this?</div> </div> </div> </div> I knew what you meant by Extended Actions. That passage in Dark Epics is the only Extended Challenges that exist in Laws books that I could find. It's why I said that it's weird and poorly implemented, and why I gave a suggestion on how you might mimic the tabletop with the standard RPS system. <br /> <br /> Heck, even just checking the BNS 2013 system I didn't find anything for Extended Actions. I would say it's probably so niche that it wasn't really something that mattered in most games, especially since RPS in both systems is so binary with the yes/no results. That or they handle it with just downtimes, you put in X downtimes and get the thing done.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1808-elmerg","name":"elmerg","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"539"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}